    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok thanks. so if it's an asset i have to depreciate it right? i'm a bit confused about how much i can actually write off in the first year vs spreading it out. also i had some other smaller stuff like desk lamps and a new monitor, can those be fully deducted right away or do they follow the same rule as the chair?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Since your chair is an asset due to its cost, it does need to be depreciated over its useful life to spread out the expense. As for your desk lamps and monitor, it depends on their costs. If they are priced at $1,000 or more, you can typically depreciate them over five years (7-year property in the U.S.) under MACRS (Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System). Desk lamps and monitors under $1,000 are usually considered to have a 5-year depreciation schedule. This allows you to write off a portion of their cost each year. The exact amount will vary depending on their purchase price and the specific tax rules that apply to your situation, so you might want to consult with a tax professional or use the appropriate depreciation method in QuickBooks.",
